From b2cbeb69c80c8fe25ef6c043704ffa2ae43cc81c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomasz Wierzbicki <tomasz.wierzbicki@example.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 17:05:27 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] feat(usage): show what prompt caching saved, per call and in
 /usage
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 💾 Cache line already reports hits and writes, but not what they are
worth. A cache read bills at a fraction of the input rate and a write at
a premium over it, so whether caching is paying off on a route depends on
the rate card, not the hit rate alone.

Each priced response now also yields the net saving: reads at the gap
between the input and cache-read rates, minus writes at the gap between
the cache-write and input rates, with context-tier rates applied to the
whole request as estimate_usage_cost does. The per-call line gains
"saved ~$0.26" (or "cost ~$0.04 extra" on a write-only first call), the
session keeps a running total that /new resets, and /usage prints it.

Subscription-included routes and rate cards without cache prices report
nothing rather than a misleading $0.00.
